<p>Cuba’s energy minister, Vicente de la O Levy, said the island had exhausted its diesel and fuel oil reserves, leaving the national electricity system in a “critical” state and worsening outages across Havana and other regions. Cuban officials blamed U.S. energy restrictions for blocking shipments. Protests broke out in and around Havana after blackouts lasted 20 to 22 hours a day in some districts. Cuban President Miguel Díaz-Canel blamed Washington for what he called a “genocidal energy blockade,” while the United States renewed a humanitarian aid offer and said Cuba’s economic management was responsible for the crisis.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/iVuhcIfASsT5YbMcPSvDmQsTdUhAUgiSIvoh8EqS2Ku8otABtI6rlyfGJ4toDeYE2RDUaV4y1wjMJRuQUwO2LcljE_I71oz6cEd1qzxWvbCl5DSE80Dkna9xiDbAdAIMaRIZdPTqf2zKwvSKcwja8QB5mobaaBhKm_yarJ9JBhkot6q8xmPy-nj7BzvaRM1LQEO9U0hEzDNnO0W4BwuhXxlBVWuY-n-wPbbPOtfoVm7v-xHWHC71hn-bhuV9jvyzZjGQiXsJ' alt='Blackouts and protests as Cuba says fuel has ‘run out’' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Economic slowdown: Semafor reported that economic activity had ground to a halt after Cuba, which relies on imported fossil fuels for most of its power, lost access to energy shipments.</li><li>Street unrest: Reuters reported seeing several mostly peaceful protest groups across Havana, describing the night as the largest single night of demonstrations in the capital since the energy crisis began in January.</li><li>Aid dispute: Washington said its $100 million aid offer should go through the Catholic Church, while Havana denied U.S. claims that it had rejected the proposal.</li><li>Supply gap: Neither Mexico nor Venezuela has supplied Cuba with fuel since President Trump’s January order, reports said, citing the current sanctions pressure.</li></ul><blockquote>We have absolutely no fuel (oil), and absolutely no diesel - Vicente de la O Levy</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuban government: Officials said electricity production had fallen to m